1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,431
A solar project can submit two plausible reports that cover the same meter and hour.

2
00:00:06,431 --> 00:00:10,718
Counting both would overstate the energy represented by those records.

3
00:00:10,718 --> 00:00:16,292
ProofGrid makes that conflict visible before evidence moves into a formal accounting process.

4
00:00:16,292 --> 00:00:24,438
It is a small, reproducible review prototype for the Renewable Energy and Energy Trading track, not a certificate issuer.

5
00:00:24,438 --> 00:00:30,656
The workspace starts with a meter label, a capacity assumption, and an hourly CSV.

6
00:00:30,656 --> 00:00:35,985
Each row contains a UTC timestamp and interval energy in kilowatt hours.

7
00:00:35,985 --> 00:00:40,871
We can use labelled synthetic examples or a processed historical reference.

8
00:00:40,871 --> 00:00:49,754
The interface exposes the candidate total and review issues, so a plausible number alone never stands in for checked evidence.

9
00:00:49,754 --> 00:00:56,689
Here, the deliberately faulty sample contains a reading above the supplied capacity and a repeated hour.

10
00:00:56,689 --> 00:01:01,457
ProofGrid explains these problems and blocks the entire batch from anchoring.

11
00:01:01,457 --> 00:01:06,225
It does not quietly discard inconvenient rows and approve the remainder.

12
00:01:06,225 --> 00:01:08,392
These checks are deterministic rules.

13
00:01:08,392 --> 00:01:15,761
A separate peak comparison can prompt human review, but it is not a trained artificial intelligence model.

14
00:01:15,761 --> 00:01:21,892
This reference comes from Open Power System Data and the CoSSMic project.

15
00:01:21,892 --> 00:01:31,599
Differences between published cumulative counters produce twenty four nominal hourly values, totaling forty point seven one nine kilowatt hours.

16
00:01:31,599 --> 00:01:38,241
These are processed historical measurements, with possible source interpolation and approximate hourly boundaries.

17
00:01:38,241 --> 00:01:45,393
The ten kilowatt capacity is our explicit demonstration assumption, not a verified installation rating.

18
00:01:45,393 --> 00:01:50,953
After inspecting the source and issues, the reviewer explicitly confirms the batch.

19
00:01:50,953 --> 00:01:56,049
ProofGrid submits a real transaction to its local Ethereum Virtual Machine.

20
00:01:56,049 --> 00:02:01,609
The contract records an evidence commitment and reserves the meter hour slots.

21
00:02:01,609 --> 00:02:04,852
The receipt demonstrates execution in this session.

22
00:02:04,852 --> 00:02:13,191
It does not authenticate a physical meter, establish renewable origin, or make this local network a public registry.

23
00:02:13,191 --> 00:02:17,763
Now we try to register the same meter hours again.

24
00:02:17,763 --> 00:02:24,163
The existing slots cause rejection, even if a repeated claim supplies different energy values.

25
00:02:24,163 --> 00:02:30,563
This protects a specific boundary: the same meter identifier in the same deployed contract.

26
00:02:30,563 --> 00:02:35,592
A renamed meter or a different registry is outside that protection.

27
00:02:35,592 --> 00:02:39,707
Trusted identity enrollment and registry integration remain future work.

28
00:02:39,707 --> 00:02:44,243
The exported package lets us check the recorded payload again.

29
00:02:44,243 --> 00:02:51,954
Verification compares its content hash with the commitment and checks the transaction against the current local contract.

30
00:02:51,954 --> 00:02:55,130
Changing a reading causes verification to fail.

31
00:02:55,130 --> 00:02:59,666
An offline matching hash alone would establish only internal consistency.

32
00:02:59,666 --> 00:03:07,377
The local receipt adds registration evidence, but neither check proves that the original measurements are physically true.

33
00:03:07,377 --> 00:03:14,380
ProofGrid brings data checks, a scoped duplicate guard, and evidence verification into one inspectable workflow.

34
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:20,916
A future pilot could measure review time and errors against an operator’s source records.

35
00:03:20,916 --> 00:03:25,584
We have not conducted that pilot or measured emissions savings.

36
00:03:25,584 --> 00:03:28,852
The chain resets with the server session.

37
00:03:28,852 --> 00:03:37,722
Our contribution is a reproducible pre review tool that could support, rather than replace, established renewable energy accounting systems.
